## 3. Important Code & Deep Dive

### 3.1 Docker Sandbox Isolation Flags (`DockerSandbox.ts`)
```typescript
const dockerArgs = [
  'run',
  '-i',                                          // Interactive stdin stream for testcases
  '--name', containerName,                       // Explicit container name for cleanup
  '--rm',                                        // Automatically remove container on exit
  '--network=none',                              // Completely disable network interfaces
  `--memory=${memoryLimitMb}m`,                  // Strict RAM allocation ceiling
  `--memory-swap=${memoryLimitMb}m`,             // Disable swap (forces immediate OOM kill)
  `--cpus=${cpuQuota}`,                          // Throttles CPU to 1.0 core
  `--pids-limit=${pidsLimit}`,                   // Prevents fork bombs by capping max PIDs
  '-v', `${normalizedHostDir}:/sandbox:rw`,      // Mount only the run directory
  '-w', '/sandbox',                              // Set execution working directory
  image,
  ...command,
];
```

*Why these specific flags matter:*
- `--network=none`: Disables the container's network stack. The only network device inside the container is loopback (`127.0.0.1`). Any attempt to make an outbound TCP/UDP connection fails instantly with `Network unreachable`.
- `--memory-swap=${limit}m`: By default, Docker allows a container to use equal amounts of disk swap when RAM is full. Setting memory-swap equal to memory disables swapping, so allocating more than 256MB triggers an immediate Out-Of-Memory (OOM) kill rather than thrashing the host disk.
- `--pids-limit=64`: If user code executes a fork bomb (`while(1) fork()`), the Linux kernel refuses to allocate more than 64 process IDs, shielding the host machine.

### 3.2 Non-Root User in Dockerfile (`docker/python/Dockerfile`)
```dockerfile
FROM python:3.11-alpine

# Create a restricted, non-root user
RUN addgroup -g 1000 sandboxgroup && \
    adduser -D -u 1000 -G sandboxgroup sandboxuser

WORKDIR /sandbox
RUN chown -R sandboxuser:sandboxgroup /sandbox

USER sandboxuser
```
*Why this matters:* If a container breakout vulnerability exists in the container runtime (runc), running as `root` (UID 0) inside the container can grant root privileges on the host. Running as non-root UID 1000 provides defense-in-depth against privilege escalation.

### 3.3 Leak-Proof Container Cleanup (`DockerSandbox.ts`)
```typescript
const timer = setTimeout(() => {
  timedOut = true;
  child.kill('SIGKILL');
  this.forceRemoveContainer(containerName);
}, timeLimitMs + 500);
```
*Why this matters:* If the host process terminates or an execution hangs, standard `--rm` containers can sometimes stay in a zombie state. Explicitly issuing `docker rm -f <containerName>` guarantees that zero containers leak in the Docker daemon.

